                        "review": "ADHD: Trackher is a single-player split-screen [strike]shoot-em up[/strike] [strike]twin-stick shooter[/strike] classic-arcade style game. At first, it's fairly simple. Shoot enemies in a top-down vertical shooter on the left, collect resources and materials and avoid danger on the right.\n\nAt the time of writing, it appears most of the gameplay takes place on the right. The player can completely ignore the left side of the screen, though not destroying enemies will significantly increase the difficulty on the right, as the enemies can actually cross from the left screen to the right, and the right screen ship has no weapons or defense of its own! Each level, or \"Tear\" is increasingly difficult, but more rewarding. Additional enemies and hazards, as well as point bonuses, appear. Tear 3 is by far the most difficult but fun experience you will have in the entire game. You continue as usual, but your screens may flip upside down, your controls may reverse, or your screens may even swap sides!\n\nAside from a few balancing acts that could enhance the game, such as allowing the left screen ship to take damage, there is a lot of fun to be had with ADHD: Trackher. The graphics are pretty, and the sound design is fairly interesting, even if it needs a bit of pollish.\n\nThis game is still clearly in early-acces, as basic features and options are missing. The game runs smooth, but is locked into (presumably) 1080p resolution. This writer's display does not support 1080p, thus the UI is either cut off or rearranged and overlaps. The gameplay is completely unaffected, however.\n\nAside from a few balancing acts (only the right-screen ship's death results in a game-over), this game really is a lot of fun! Once you top the leaderboards, however, there is no incentive to keep playing. Starting any game will completely clear your current leaderboard position. The game does use this as a challenge fairly well, but if you lack the confidence to top your old high score, you're better off creating a new Steam account and purchasing the game again if you don't want to risk losing your bragging rights. Personally, I've never been one for leaderboard domination, so I have no problem starting fresh again each time to explore what the game has to offer.\n\nSlight issues like this are to be expected from early-access titles, so there is absolutley no harm in givng this game a shot. ADHD: Trackher is very much worth the $8 price tag. Give it a try if you're a fan of classic arcade games or 2D shooters! I'll probably buy this game a second time when the Nintendo Switch port drops!\n\n[i]P.S: Don't cheat by playing \"co-op.\" :P",
